          <Pad Id="V4xdqHYLfJGEevS2DdiuaV" Comment="Title" Bounds="182,781,190,19" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="HowTo Draw Splines">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="7JEeFEjdP7FXQ08fOykqIC" Bounds="85,880,1714,167" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Notes:&#xA;- All curves use the same Points. They are Point nodes (circle with Drag) in a ForEach region, drag them in the Renderer. Drag two Points close together to see Alpha 0 overshoot.&#xA;- CatmullRom goes through every Point. BSpline takes the Points as a control polygon: the curve starts at the first and ends at the last Point (open, clamped) and only passes near the others.&#xA;- Alpha is the knot parametrization of CatmullRom: 0 uniform (every segment gets the same time, unevenly spaced points overshoot or loop), 0.5 centripetal (the default, no loops or cusps), 1 chordal (the time of a segment follows its length).&#xA;- Open ends have tangent 0 for every Alpha, like d3's curveCatmullRom. For Alpha 0 this differs from the classic end tangent (P2 - P1) / 2, but avoids a jump between Alpha 0 and a value just above it.&#xA;- Tension 1 turns CatmullRom into straight lines. Closed joins the last Point to the first with a smooth transition.&#xA;- Fewer than two Points leave the Input path unchanged. Both nodes add exact cubics to a QtPath; a path must fit one command to Qt (about 65 000 bytes, see HowTo Draw a Path).">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
